{
  "rationale": "The spec requires >=1 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-10 while minimising area. Both allowed families compute identical output bits for the same data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding. However, unrolled_k instantiates k parallel micro-rotation datapaths, so for any given accuracy it has strictly larger area than iterative. Since the throughput constraint is only 1 MSPS, iterative with N up to 22 and typical Fmax easily exceeds this (e.g. Fmax/25 > 1 MHz). Therefore unrolled_k cannot appear on the min-area Pareto front and is not worth budget. We focus 100% of the first-round budget on iterative to map the area-accuracy trade-off. The ranges cover the likely feasible region: data_width >=12 is required because W=11 output quantisation alone can exceed the error bound; n_iter 10..22 covers convergence and truncation-error needs; angle_guard and frac_guard are varied to find the minimal area that still meets accuracy; rounding is included because it can improve accuracy at low area cost.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Iterative is the lowest-area family for any given accuracy because it shares a single datapath across all N micro-rotations. The throughput constraint is easily met. The parameter ranges are chosen to explore the low-area corner (small W, small N, small guards) while also covering enough range to map the front up to higher accuracy.",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 20
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 10,
          "high": 22,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": -2,
          "high": 2,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        }
      ],
      "family": "iterative",
      "budget_share": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "This is the final round, so only stop/infeasible are available. Infeasible is ruled out: 42 of 60 evaluations are feasible, with 0% violating throughput_msps >= 1 (best seen 12.4 MSPS vs the 1 MSPS floor) and the accuracy constraint met by the whole front (best max_abs_err 1.79e-05 = 2^-15.77, far under the 2^-10 limit). The feasible Pareto front is well-populated (12 designs) and spans area_um2 7714..10057 against the HV reference 40000, and accuracy_bits 10.6..15.8 against the HV reference 10 — i.e. it already covers the useful trade-off region, with the selection rule's pick (min area_um2) being the iterative [data_width=17 n_iter=14 angle_guard=-2 frac_guard=1 rounding=trunc] design at 7714 um^2, 10.6 accuracy bits, 7.7 MSPS, comfortably satisfying both constraints. The only unexplored family (unrolled_k) cannot beat the iterative corner on area for this low-area spec, since it shares the same datapath but adds chained-rotation hardware; the front's area floor is already set by the smallest feasible iterative configuration. No further improvement is expected within the remaining budget, so I stop on the current front." 
